BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khoj-mozilla-counterstructures-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Khoj x Mozilla Foundation: Counterstructures Residency 20
 26 (Delhi)
DESCRIPTION:Counterstructures is Mozilla Foundation's cultural R&D residenc
 y\, in which creative technologists prototype alternatives to dominant tec
 h systems: cultural artifacts\, installations and tools that question and 
 reframe them. Each edition operationalises the findings of a regional part
 icipatory assembly into thematic tracks\; the Delhi edition\, hosted by Kh
 oj International Artists' Association\, works from Mozilla's assemblies wi
 th G5A in Delhi and Mumbai and runs on two tracks. RETAINING CULTURAL AUTH
 ORITY asks what happens when oral\, embodied or community-held knowledge i
 s converted into datasets and machine-readable forms\, which cultural mate
 rials become visible to AI systems and which do not\, and how communities 
 can retain authority over what is digitised\, interpreted\, reused or deli
 berately kept private. REMEMBERING AND THINKING WITH MACHINES asks where t
 hought resides when people think with machines\, at what stage human judgm
 ent enters the system\, and what cultural or professional knowledge is los
 t when judgment is standardised through automation. Proposals may respond 
 to one or both tracks and can take many forms\, but must critically counte
 r the questions the tracks raise rather than simply illustrate them. WHAT 
 RESIDENTS DO: prototype an artifact\, installation\, system\, artwork or t
 ool for public exhibition\; take part in listening\, reading\, workshops a
 nd facilitated critique\, testing ideas with peers and community members\;
  write a 750-word report on the project's development and insights for the
  Counterstructures report and online archive\; and present the work public
 ly. WHO IT IS FOR: creative technologists and multidisciplinary artists us
 ing technology as a medium for cultural inquiry\, bringing critical perspe
 ctives and research-driven practice. Experience with AI can be early-stage
  or advanced\, but SOME PROFICIENCY WITH CODING IS REQUIRED. ELIGIBILITY: 
 this Delhi edition is open to Indian nationals only. Earlier editions ran 
 with tiat in San Francisco (responding to Hollywood's 8 Rules for AI) and 
 with Rhizome in New York\, whose residents showed at the New Museum. APPLY
  through the Mozilla Foundation form at https://mozillafoundation.tfaforms
 .net/f/khojresidency\; the guiding principles for the call are set out in 
 the form. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Khoj International 
 Artists' Association in collaboration with Mozilla Foundation\nLocation: N
 ew Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIONALS ONLY
 .\nAward: Mozilla's Creative Futures: Counterstructures programme publishe
 s USD 2\,500 per resident (USD 2\,000 stipend plus a USD 500 materials bud
 get)\, along with weekly in-person work sessions\, guest lectures from pra
 ctitioners across art\, technology and community design\, and publication 
 of the finished project in the collective documentation report. CAVEAT: ne
 ither the Khoj call page nor the Mozilla application form restates a figur
 e for this six-week Delhi edition\, and the published package covers the t
 en-week format\, so confirm the amount with Khoj before applying. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: Six weeks\, 1 October to 12 November 2026\, running
  weekly in Delhi\, with final projects presented publicly 13 to 15 Novembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstru
 ctures-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstructures-residen
 cy/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIO
 NALS ONLY.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:algorave-istanbul-open-call-artists@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Algorave Istanbul: Open Call for New Artists and Performe
 rs
DESCRIPTION:UNPAID COMMUNITY CALL. Algorave Istanbul\, the local node of th
 e international algorave scene (live-coded music and visuals performed for
  an audience)\, is looking for new artists to create\, experiment and perf
 orm with. The call names live coding\, sound\, visuals\, audiovisual perfo
 rmance\, and generative or reactive systems\, and says explicitly that it 
 is open to formats the organisers have not thought of yet. WHO CAN APPLY: 
 artists based in Istanbul. There is no stated experience requirement and n
 o selection process described. Applications close 30 September 2026. WHAT 
 IS OFFERED: performance opportunities and collaboration within the collect
 ive. No fee\, stipend or expenses are mentioned anywhere in the call\, so 
 treat this as unpaid unless the organisers say otherwise. APPLY through th
 e Google Form\, or email algoraveistanbul@gmail.com. The call is published
  in Turkish and English.\n\nOrganisation: Algorave Istanbul\nLocation: Ist
 anbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.\nAward: UNPAID\, an
 d no figure of any kind is published. The call states no fee\, honorarium\
 , stipend or expenses\; it is an invitation to join a local live-coding co
 mmunity and perform with it. Listed here as a deliberate exception to the 
 desk's paid-only rule because it is one of very few live-coding openings i
 n the region. No application fee.\nDuration: Open-ended involvement with t
 he collective\; no fixed term or number of performances stated.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS1bxe
 3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS
 1bxe3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Istanbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-new-york-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: New York Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's New York Fellowship puts a cross-d
 isciplinary cohort of artists\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia 
 in the United States for six months to live with a new cultural landscape:
  attending performances\, exhibitions\, workshops and discussions\, and bu
 ilding relationships with peers and with ACC's alumni network. It is delib
 erately an extended pause from daily work with NO obligation to produce or
  present anything. Applicants must be citizens or legal permanent resident
 s of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: Afghanistan\, Banglade
 sh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kon
 g SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, 
 Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lank
 a\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): a
 rchitecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeology\, art history\, ar
 ts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\, da
 nce\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, theat
 er and visual art\; video art and photography apply under visual art. Addi
 tional requirements: at least 21 years old by the start of the fellowship\
 , at least five years of professional experience in an eligible discipline
 \, no paid work\, university coursework or teaching (online included) duri
 ng the fellowship\, and enough confident English to handle daily life inde
 pendently\, as the J-1 visa requires. Previous New York Fellows cannot rea
 pply\, and anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years
  is ineligible. VISA: fellows must enter the U.S. on an ACC-sponsored J-1 
 visa with no exceptions\, which bars paid work of any kind during the fell
 owship\, including work for a non-U.S. employer and participation in ticke
 ted events even unpaid. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: New York City a
 nd the wider United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents
  of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citize
 ns and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 38\,000 for 
 the six-month fellowship\, including a USD 3\,000 advance for travel costs
 . ACC deducts certain pre-arrival costs from the grant: the USD 220 SEVIS 
 fee\, mandatory travel health insurance (roughly USD 675 to 800) and Inter
 national House housing if you take it (roughly USD 19\,400 to 20\,600 for 
 six months). Fellows can expect USD 10\,000 to 31\,100 in hand after arriv
 al depending on insurance and housing choices. Also included: ACC-organise
 d group activities and institutional visits\, individual programmatic and 
 logistical support\, and entry to the ACC alumni network. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Six months\, in one of two cohorts: 1 January to 30 June 20
 28\, or 1 July to 31 December 2028. ACC assigns the cohort.\n\nMore: https
 ://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City and the wider United States. Open to citizens and le
 gal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and region
 s in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-individual-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Individual Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's Individual Fellowship funds artist
 s\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia and the U.S. to spend one to
  six months somewhere new or less familiar\, pursuing self-designed resear
 ch and cultural immersion: meeting practitioners\, visiting studios\, muse
 ums\, galleries\, theatres and libraries\, joining workshops and symposia\
 , and doing archival research. ACC is explicit that this is not a producti
 on residency\, not a conference travel grant and not a PR tour. APPLYING J
 OINTLY: two or three people who share research goals can apply together as
  equal partners (one primary applicant\, up to two co-applicants\, no assi
 stant roles)\; the USD 35\,000 cap covers the whole team. Applicants must 
 be citizens or legal permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, terr
 itory or region: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, C
 hina (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\,
  Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakist
 an\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. an
 d Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and t
 heory)\, archaeology\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\
 , conservation\, crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, lite
 rature\, museum studies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and p
 hotography apply under visual art. Additional requirements: at least 21 ye
 ars old by the start\, at least five years of professional experience in a
 n eligible discipline\, no university coursework or teaching during the fe
 llowship\, and confident English if travelling to the U.S. on a J-1 visa. 
 Anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years is ineligi
 ble. DESTINATIONS: any eligible country except the one you currently live 
 in\; a place you have lived in for over a year or hold citizenship in need
 s a strong rationale. U.S. fellowships need at least 30 consecutive days a
 nd cannot start before 1 October 2027 because of J-1 processing\; fellowsh
 ips in Asia need 30 cumulative days\, splittable into trips of at least 14
  consecutive days with no more than 14 days between them. ACC sponsors the
  J-1 for U.S. travel\; for Asia you arrange your own visas\, and the fees 
 can go in the budget. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-ex
 change and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documentary
 \, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running a
  concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and full-
 time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only to 
 travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:5
 9am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of
  recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novembe
 r 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded 
 activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply
  through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Appl
 icants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\
 n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: Travel between an
 d within Asia and the United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent 
 residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions\, including 
 the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place where you currently live.\
 nAward: Up to USD 35\,000\, the same cap whether you apply alone or jointl
 y with up to two co-applicants. Covers economy airfare\, ground transport\
 , accommodation\, food and daily living costs\, visa fees\, mandatory trav
 el health insurance\, interpretation and costs tied to the exchange activi
 ties. Also included: ACC logistical and research guidance\, introductions 
 to alumni and professionals\, and visa assistance for U.S. travel. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One to six months\, at least 30 days in the destin
 ation\; ACC prefers two months or more in a single place.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Travel between and within Asia and the United States. Open to citi
 zens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories
  and regions\, including the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place w
 here you currently live.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-graduate-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Graduate Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:ACC's Graduate Fellowship covers travel and living costs for a 
 small number of graduate students from Asia who come to the U.S. because c
 omparable arts and culture programmes are unavailable or underrepresented 
 at home. The five-year professional experience requirement that applies to
  ACC's other programmes is WAIVED here. Applicants must be citizens or leg
 al permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: A
 fghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, 
 East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Ma
 cau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\,
  Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBL
 E DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeolo
 gy\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, c
 rafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum st
 udies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and photography apply u
 nder visual art. Additional requirements: be pursuing a graduate degree in
  one of the 16 eligible disciplines\; be enrolled full-time and in residen
 ce at a U.S. graduate programme\, or hold proof of admission by 1 April 20
 27\; be in the coursework phase\, not the research or thesis phase\; and c
 omplete at least two consecutive semesters of full-time coursework within 
 the grant period. Part-time\, summer-only\, online and non-degree programm
 es are not eligible. ACC does not sponsor student visas\, so the F-1 or eq
 uivalent is between you and your university. Renewal for a second year dep
 ends on ACC's review of your academic record and proof of continued enrolm
 ent. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:59am ET\, applications
  close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of recommendation are d
 ue 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs November 2026 to April 2027\
 , notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded activity must take pl
 ace between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply through the GoApply 
 portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Applicants may apply to o
 nly one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Asia
 n Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: United States\, at the applicant's own
  graduate institution. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents of A
 CC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens an
 d U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 15\,000 for one a
 cademic year\, renewable once for a second year at ACC's discretion. Cover
 s travel and living expenses during the coursework phase only\, NOT tuitio
 n and NOT research costs such as fieldwork or equipment. Also included: in
 troductions into ACC's global alumni network. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: One academic year of full-time coursework (Fall 2027 to Spring 2028\, o
 r Spring 2028 to Fall 2028)\, renewable for a second year.\n\nMore: https:
 //www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\, at the applicant's own graduate institution. Open 
 to citizens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, terr
 itories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents a
 re NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-organization-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Organization Grant 2027
DESCRIPTION:ACC's Organization Grant funds arts and culture organisations i
 n Asia and the U.S. to run cultural-exchange activities for their staff\, 
 communities and collaborators: sending an activity lead and participants a
 broad\, hosting incoming participants at home\, or both. Up to USD 50\,000
 \, set by ACC against the proposed budget\, activities\, travel duration a
 nd application quality. ELIGIBILITY: the applicant must be a nonprofit\, a
  government-affiliated or sponsored arts or cultural institution (national
  museums\, performing arts centres and the like)\, or an academic or highe
 r-education institution\; legally registered and based in an ACC-eligible 
 country\, territory or region\; and have at least five years of artistic o
 r cultural programming experience. Each application names a primary contac
 t\, an activity lead who runs the work on the ground (can be the same pers
 on)\, and activity participants who may be staff or external collaborators
 . The lead and all participants must be citizens or legal permanent reside
 nts of an eligible location\, and they do not have to share a nationality 
 with each other or with the organisation. Applicants must be citizens or l
 egal permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region:
  Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\
 , East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, 
 Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines
 \, Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGI
 BLE DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeo
 logy\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\,
  crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum 
 studies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and photography apply
  under visual art. TRAVEL: one to six months within the grant period\, at 
 least 30 consecutive days in the U.S. or 30 cumulative days in Asia (split
 table into trips of at least 14 consecutive days\, no more than 14 days ap
 art). Travel to a place the lead or participants have spent over a year in
  needs a strong rationale\, and multi-destination or short itineraries are
  less competitive. ACC does not sponsor visas for this programme\; the org
 anisation secures them. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: Asia and the Un
 ited States. The applicant organisation must be legally registered and bas
 ed in an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region\; activity leads and p
 articipants must be citizens or permanent residents of an eligible locatio
 n\, not necessarily the same one as the organisation.\nAward: Up to USD 50
 \,000. Covers economy airfare\, ground transport\, accommodation\, food an
 d daily living costs\, visa fees\, mandatory travel health insurance\, int
 erpretation and costs tied to the exchange activities. Also included: ACC 
 logistical and substantive guidance\, introductions to alumni\, arts profe
 ssionals and institutions\, and invitations to local ACC events. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: One to six months of travel\; at least 30 consecutiv
 e days for U.S. activity\, or 30 cumulative days in Asia.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Asia and the United States. The applicant organisation must be leg
 ally registered and based in an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region
 \; activity leads and participants must be citizens or permanent residents
  of an eligible location\, not necessarily the same one as the organisatio
 n.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tokyo-biennale-2027-social-dive@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tokyo Biennale 2027: SOCIAL DIVE open call for artists an
 d curators
DESCRIPTION:SOCIAL DIVE is Tokyo Biennale's open call\, now in its third ed
 ition and\, for 2027\, open to both international and domestic applicants 
 (the 2019 round drew roughly 1\,500 overseas applications and the 2025 int
 ernational-only round roughly 1\,400). Artists and curators propose projec
 ts to be presented as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027\, whose theme is 'From M
 e to Us'. THEME: general producer Masato Nakamura frames the biennale agai
 nst an age of conflict and division\, arguing that to avoid repeating memo
 ries of war\, violence and exclusion we must reexamine the wisdom and cult
 ural resources passed down across generations. Tokyo carries the memories 
 of neighbourhoods dating back to the Edo period\, twice suffered great los
 s through the Great Kanto Earthquake and the Second World War\, and each t
 ime was rebuilt through the strength of its citizens\; it is also an inter
 national city where different languages\, memories\, beliefs\, wounds and 
 hopes intersect. Quoting Kenzaburo Oe\, 'I cannot live my life over again.
  But we can live our lives over again'\, the call proposes that when a sin
 gle 'me' encounters others and reconnects relationships\, a new consciousn
 ess of 'us' emerges. Art projects are treated as a form of cultural prescr
 iption\, reconnecting people with people\, people with places and people w
 ith memories. ELIGIBILITY: open to artists\, curators\, researchers\, coll
 ectives and practitioners in related fields active in Japan and abroad\; a
 pplicants must be 18 or older at the time of application\; there are no re
 strictions on nationality\, place of residence or base of operations\; par
 ticipants must be able to work in Tokyo during the designated period. ACCE
 PTED FORMATS: artwork creation\, exhibitions\, performances\, video works\
 , tours\, workshops\, participatory projects\, research projects and curat
 orial projects. Applications cover both artwork production by artists and 
 art project proposals by curators. APPLY via the Google Form linked from s
 ocialdive.jp/en (a Google account is required) during the application peri
 od 17 July to 30 September 2026\, 23:59 JST. Read the official call docume
 ntation for full eligibility requirements and guidelines.\n\nOrganisation:
  Tokyo Biennale\nLocation: Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out 
 residency-based production\, research\, meetings\, preparation and impleme
 ntation in Tokyo during a designated period. No restrictions on nationalit
 y\, place of residence or base of operations.\nAward: Travel up to JPY 300
 \,000 (economy class\, one person maximum)\, accommodation in Tokyo\, a pr
 oject development budget of up to JPY 200\,000\, and a JPY 100\,000 honora
 rium. Additional support includes venue coordination\, interpretation\, tr
 anslation and an invitation letter. No application fee.\nDuration: Residen
 cy-based work in Tokyo during a designated period\, with the project prese
 nted as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027.\n\nMore: https://socialdive.jp/en/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out residency-based pro
 duction\, research\, meetings\, preparation and implementation in Tokyo du
 ring a designated period. No restrictions on nationality\, place of reside
 nce or base of operations.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-fragmented-distance-2026@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T193233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
SUMMARY:Deadline: Resynthesising the Traditional: Fragmented Distance (CTM 
 x NOPA Artistic Lab\, Armenia)
DESCRIPTION:Fragmented Distance is the latest artistic lab from CTM Festiva
 l's Resynthesising the Traditional initiative (co-curated by Stas Shariful
 la and CTM)\, taking place 1 to 10 October 2026 in Armenia in collaboratio
 n with NOPA\, a festival dedicated to sonic arts and experimental music. R
 esynthesising the Traditional explores how musicians\, sound artists\, and
  researchers can critically engage with traditions and heritage\, using re
 synthesis (a computer-music method that analyses sounds to extract their f
 undamental components) as a guiding metaphor\, and resisting conservative 
 views of culture as frozen or untouchable\; the lab is an open toolbox hel
 ping artists connect to 'the traditional' outside of exoticisation\, cultu
 ral essentialism\, and uneven power dynamics. This edition focuses on dias
 pora\, memory\, displacement\, transmission\, and hybridity\, responding t
 o Armenia's shift into a point of arrival for refugees and diasporic commu
 nities and to the displacement following the ethnic cleansing of Artsakh /
  Nagorno-Karabakh. It invites artists whose work resynthesising traditiona
 l practices addresses how traditional musical forms are maintained\, alter
 ed\, or recontextualised as they move across geographies and generations\,
  how in-betweenness and non-belonging can be articulated through sound\, a
 nd how sonic expression can act as rehumanisation. THE LAB (eight days\, 1
 0:00-19:00 daily) includes theory and practice workshops\, individual clos
 ed oral presentations\, solo public performances (15-20 minutes)\, special
 ist inputs\, guided sonic improvisations\, field trips\, and feedback sess
 ions. Following the lab\, participants may propose new solo or collaborati
 ve work to the Resynthesising the Traditional team\; selected works are sh
 ortlisted for commission and presentation at CTM Festival Berlin and/or NO
 PA Festival Yerevan. CONDITIONS: solo artists/projects only\; applicants s
 hould have an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional id
 eas and sounds through a contemporary lens\, and clear next steps\; workin
 g knowledge of English recommended. SELECTION: a pre-selection committee (
 Varoujan Chetirian\, Stas Sharifulla\, Taica Replansky) creates a shortlis
 t\, from which up to six participants are chosen by lab co-hosts Cinna Pey
 ghamy\, Vardan Baghdasaryan\, and Varoujan Chetirian. TIMELINE: deadline 8
  August 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; results by early September 2026\; lab 1 to 10
  October 2026. Apply with a portfolio (PDF\, max 10 pages) and an audio li
 nk\; questions to team@nopa.art. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: CTM Festi
 val with NOPA Festival\nLocation: Yerevan / Armenia. Preference to artists
  who reside in or have a diasporic connection to Armenia\, Georgia\, Iran\
 , Iraq\, Syria\, the Anatolian region\, the Caspian Sea\, the Levant\, and
  the Northern Caucasus.\nAward: Participants supported with a travel budge
 t of up to EUR 450 each\, accommodation and catering for the duration of t
 he lab\, a NOPA Festival pass\, and a per diem. Visa fees supported when r
 equired\, and letters of invitation provided. Free to apply.\nDuration: Ei
 ght-day lab\, 1 to 10 October 2026 (arrival 1 October\, departure 10 Octob
 er)\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/activities/resynthesising-the-tra
 ditional/artistic-labs/artistic-labs/open-call-nopa-festival?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/activities/resynthesising-the-traditional/a
 rtistic-labs/artistic-labs/open-call-nopa-festival?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Yerevan / Armenia. Preference to artists who reside in or have a d
 iasporic connection to Armenia\, Georgia\, Iran\, Iraq\, Syria\, the Anato
 lian region\, the Caspian Sea\, the Levant\, and the Northern Caucasus.
END:VEVENT
